As there has been discussion how monster generation is affected I 
decided to tackele that part of the code. It was a little bit bigger 
project so that's why it took me three days to get it ready. Hopefully I 
get the next instalment out faster.

So here goes:

HOW RANDOM MONSTER IS SELECTED

This part of code is in file makemon.c starting from line 1191.

1191: static NEARDATA struct {
1192:    int choice_count;
1193:    char mchoices[SPECIAL_PM];    /* value range is 0..127 */
1194: } rndmonst_state = { -1, {0} };
1195:

Here is a declaration of a structured variable rndmonst_state that is 
used to hold the frequences of the eligible monster types. The 
choice_count holds the sum of the frequences if the structure is valid 
and the array mchoises holds the individual frequences. Negative 
choice_count value means that the table should be reconstructed. 
choice_count is intialized to -1 and mchoises to 0.

1196: /* select a random monster type */
1197: struct permonst *
1198: rndmonst()

Here is the function rndmonst() and it starts with a function header 
that tells the return value (here pointer to a monster type) and no 
parameters. The return value is a pointer to the selected random monster 
type or a null pointer if no monster type could be selected.

1199: {

This starts the function body.

1200:    register struct permonst *ptr;
1201:    register int mndx, ct;

Three additional variables are declared:
ptr is a pointer to a monster type,
mndx  is an integer which is used as a index to mons array that holds 
all the monster type entries and
ct is an  integer.

1202:    if (u.uz.dnum == quest_dnum && rn2(7) && (ptr = qt_montype()) != 0)
1203:       return ptr;

If the character is on one of the quest levels, then six out of seven 
monsters generated are quest monsters. Quest monster selection is done 
by function qt_montype() described below. If qt_montype() succesfully 
selected a monster type return that as the selected monster type.

1204:    if (rndmonst_state.choice_count < 0) {    /* need to recalculate */

Here starts the reconstruction of the rndmonst_state structure. There is 
a function reset_rndmonst() (described below) that sets the choice_count 
to negative value if needed to force reconstruction of the 
rndmonst_state structure.

1205:       int zlevel, minmlev, maxmlev;
1206:       boolean elemlevel;

Four new variables are declared:
zlevel is an integer to hold the level difficulty,
minmlev is the lower bound for the monster level under selection,
maxmlev is the upper bound for the monster level under selection and
elemlevel is a flag that tells the selection is done on an elemental plane.

1207: #ifdef REINCARNATION
1208:       boolean upper;
1209: #endif
1210:

Here is a block of code that is conditionally compiled in. So if 
REINCARNATION is set, ie. the rogue-level is compiled into the game, 
then one additional new variable is declared:
upper is a flag telling that the selection is valid only for monster 
types that are represented with uppercase charcters.

1211:       rndmonst_state.choice_count = 0;

The sum of eligible monster type frequences is set to 0.

1212:       /* look for first common monster */
1213:       for (mndx = LOW_PM; mndx < SPECIAL_PM; mndx++) {

Loop through the monster types starting with the lowest type entry entry 
until the special monster type entries start.

1214:          if (!uncommon(mndx)) break;

The function uncommon() (described below) checks if the monster type is 
eligible for random generation. It returns true if the monster type is 
uncommon ie. not eligible for random generation. So if the return value 
is false then mndx is an index to the first monster type entry that is 
eligible for random creation and the loop is aborted early.

1215:          rndmonst_state.mchoices[mndx] = 0;

The monster type is not eligible for random generation so its generation 
frequency is set to 0.

1216:       }

The end of the loop.

1217:       if (mndx == SPECIAL_PM) {
1218:       /* evidently they've all been exterminated */

If the loop ended with the index reaching the upper bound then there is 
no eligible monster types for random generation.

1219: #ifdef DEBUG
1220:          pline("rndmonst: no common mons!");
1221: #endif

Another conditional compilation block. If the game is compiled for 
debugging purposes a message is shown. This conditional compilation is 
quite common in the code. Give a message telling why nothing happened so 
that while debugging one does not wonder if it was caused by normal 
behavior or a bug.

1222:          return (struct permonst *)0;

As no monster type was eligible to random generation a null pointer is 
returned.

1223:       } /* else `mndx' now ready for use below */

OK,. at least one monster type is eligible for generation.

1224:       zlevel = level_difficulty();

The function level_difficulty() is described below.

1225:       /* determine the level of the weakest monster to make. */
1226:       minmlev = zlevel / 6;

The lower bound for the monster level.

1227:       /* determine the level of the strongest monster to make. */
1228:       maxmlev = (zlevel + u.ulevel) / 2;

The upper bound for the monster level.

1229: #ifdef REINCARNATION
1230:       upper = Is_rogue_level(&u.uz);
1231: #endif

Conditional compilation block. If the rogue-level is compiled into the 
game then upper is set if the character is on the rogue-level.

1232:       elemlevel = In_endgame(&u.uz) && !Is_astralevel(&u.uz);
1233:

If the character is in the endgame but not on the astral plane then she 
is on an elemental plane.

1234: /*
1235:  *    Find out how many monsters exist in the range we have selected.
1236:  */
1237:       /* (`mndx' initialized above) */
1238:       for ( ; mndx < SPECIAL_PM; mndx++) {

Another loop using mndx as its index but in this case mndx is already 
intialized above. Loop until the special monster type entries start.

1239:          ptr = &mons[mndx];
1240:          rndmonst_state.mchoices[mndx] = 0;

ptr now points to the monster type entry and the generation frequence of 
that type is set to 0.

1241:          if (tooweak(mndx, minmlev) || toostrong(mndx, maxmlev))
1242              continue;

If the monster type is too weak or too strong based on the lower and 
upper bounds then skip it. The macros tooweak() and toostrong() are 
described in file makemon.c on lines 38 and 39 as:

38: #define toostrong(monindx, lev) (monstr[monindx] > lev)
39: #define tooweak(monindx, lev)  (monstr[monindx] < lev)

monstr array is the monster difficulty array which is generated by 
makedefs from the monster type information during the compilation. How 
the monster difficulty is calculated is described below.

1243: #ifdef REINCARNATION
1244:          if (upper && !isupper(def_monsyms[(int)(ptr->mlet)])) 
continue;
1245: #endif

If the character is on rogue-level and the monster type is not 
represented by a uppercase character then skip it.

1246:          if (elemlevel && wrong_elem_type(ptr)) continue;

If the character is on an elemental plane then skip any monster types 
that don't belong there. The function wrong_elem_type() is described below.

The code on lines 1241 to 1246 shows why to use such precalculated 
values as minmlev, maxmlev, upper and elemlevel. They speed up the loop 
as they are not calculated each time through the loop. upper and 
elemlevel serve another purpose also: the conditions on lines 1244 and 
1246 don't waste time checking the monster type if the reason for that 
check is false.

In the programming language C a conditional expression's evaluation is 
stopped when the value of that expression is clear. This means that an 
expression like 'A and B' does not evaluate B if A is false nor in 'A or 
B' if A is true.

1247:          if (uncommon(mndx)) continue;

No uncommon monster types so skip it.

1248:          if (Inhell && (ptr->geno & G_NOHELL)) continue;

If the character is in hell then skip the monster types that are not 
eligible for hell.

1249:          ct = (int)(ptr->geno & G_FREQ) + align_shift(ptr);

Set ct to the frequency of  the monster type adjusted by the alignment 
of the level. The function align_shift() returns a value in the range 
0-5 by comparing the alignment of the monster type with the alignment of 
the level.

1250:          if (ct < 0 || ct > 127)
1251:             panic("rndmonst: bad count [#%d: %d]", mndx, ct);

This shouldn't happen but if monster types are added or adjusted then 
errors might creep in. Notice that the error message given reflects the 
name of the variable not the function of the variable. Bad practise!

1252:          rndmonst_state.choice_count += ct;
1253:          rndmonst_state.mchoices[mndx] = (char)ct;

Add the frequency to the sum of frequencies and set it on that monster type.

1254:       }

The end of the loop.

1255: /*
1256:  *        Possible modification:  if choice_count is "too low",
1257:  *        expand minmlev..maxmlev range and try again.
1258:  */

A note for furher development.

1259:    } /* choice_count+mchoices[] recalc */
1260:

The end of the frequency table construction.

1261:    if (rndmonst_state.choice_count <= 0) {
1262:       /* maybe no common mons left, or all are too weak or too 
strong */
1263: #ifdef DEBUG
